WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
Experiment tracking.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:08.000
The most important question in applied ML is which model was that, and how do I reproduce it.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.533
Without tracking you get the folder graveyard.

4
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:15.167
Folders named final version two, and model best real.

5
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:18.000
Within a month, nobody knows which one was which.

6
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.601
With tracking, the answer is a query.

7
00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:22.467
Without it, it is archaeology.

8
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:25.333
A run is the atomic unit.

9
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:30.533
Anything that influenced the result is attached to it, by code, not by memory.

10
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:32.300
Parameters and config.

11
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.200
Metrics, like loss curves and eval scores.

12
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.467
The git commit.

13
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:37.833
The data version.

14
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.367
And artifacts, like weights and plots.

15
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:44.700
Once runs are tracked, they become a table you can query.

16
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.543
Sort by the metric, and the best run floats to the top.

17
00:00:48.543 --> 00:00:50.733
Hand a colleague the exact run ID.

18
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:54.267
Here is the same idea in MLflow.

19
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:58.067
We loop over three learning rates, and each one gets its own run.

20
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:02.000
We log the parameters, and after training, the validation score.

21
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.600
Then we attach a plot as an artifact.

22
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.667
The UI then lists all three runs side by side.

23
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:11.800
A teammate asks why March's model beats June's.

24
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.633
Because every retrain was a tracked run, you compare them directly.

25
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:20.400
Same code, same hyperparameters, but June logged a newer data version.

26
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.037
That version included a noisy detector.

27
00:01:23.037 --> 00:01:25.733
Three minutes, instead of a week of guessing.

28
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:27.809
Let's recap.

29
00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:29.633
A run is the atomic unit.

30
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:33.433
Log parameters, metrics, code, data, and artifacts.

31
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:36.100
Tracked runs become a table you can query.

32
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.802
That habit separates engineering from notebook science.

33
00:01:39.802 --> 00:01:41.533
Next up: the model registry.
